Kurokesu rpicam-apps packaging notes
====================================

This package is a Kurokesu fork of Raspberry Pi's rpicam-apps
packaging. The recipe lives on a single DEP-14 `debian/latest` branch
and reuses RPi's recipe verbatim where possible. Deviations are kept
minimal and tracked here so they can be retired when no longer needed.

rpicam-apps is built against Kurokesu's libcamera fork, pinned to a
specific release at build time (see "Pinned libcamera" below).

Supported suites
----------------

  * trixie: build with `--profiles=nodoc`.
  * bookworm: build with `--profiles=pkg.rpicam-apps.nolibav,nodoc`
    and the `~bpo12+1` version suffix.

The `pkg.rpicam-apps.nolibav` profile drops the libav (ffmpeg) encoder
on bookworm. See "Per-suite build profile" below.

Branch layout
-------------

This branch carries the packaging recipe and the GitHub Actions
workflows that exercise it. It is an orphan branch. It shares no
history with the source `kurokesu` branch and contains no source code.

  * `debian/`            - the recipe.
  * `.github/workflows/` - `_build.yml` (reusable build),
                           `ci.yml` (transient validation),
                           `release.yml` (durable publish).
  * `.github/keys/`      - pinned RPi and Debian apt archive keyrings
                           and the Kurokesu archive keyring.
  * `release.sh`         - paired-tag release helper.

The workflow files live here, rather than on the repository's default
branch, because GitHub Actions resolves `pull_request` and tag-push
triggers from the base branch's workflow files. Hosting them on
`kurokesu` would prevent PRs targeting `debian/latest` from triggering
CI.

The source tree (the C++ code plus Kurokesu's deltas) lives on the
`kurokesu` branch and is checked out separately at build time. The
orig tarball is produced from `git archive` of that branch.

Identity adaptations
--------------------

These define Kurokesu's fork:

  * Version scheme: `1:<upstream>+krks<N>-<M>`, for example
    `1:1.12.0+krks1-1`. The leading `1:` is a Debian epoch that makes
    Kurokesu's rpicam-apps outrank Raspberry Pi's stock build even during
    the release-lag window before we rebase onto a newer RPi version. The
    `+krks<N>` counter resets on each RPi resync. `-<M>` is the Debian
    packaging revision. The epoch is comparison-only: it appears in the
    changelog and the built .deb but never in git tags or asset
    filenames.
    Retire when: never (the epoch is permanent; removing it is itself a
    downgrade).
  * `Maintainer`, `Uploaders` and `Vcs-*` repointed to
    github.com/Kurokesu/rpicam-apps.
    Retire when: never (defines the fork).

Pinned libcamera
----------------

rpicam-apps links against libcamera, so each rpicam-apps release is
built against a specific Kurokesu/libcamera release rather than
whatever the build host happens to resolve.

  * `debian/libcamera.pin` holds the Kurokesu/libcamera packaging
    version on a single line, for example
    `0.7.1+rpt20260429+krks1-4`. It is kept epoch-free: it feeds the
    epoch-free libcamera source tag and release-asset glob. This is the
    compatibility-matrix entry, recorded in git and fixed per rpicam-apps
    tag. Bumping libcamera is a deliberate, reviewed commit that edits
    this file.
  * The `Pin libcamera from release` step in `_build.yml` is gated on
    this file's presence. It downloads that exact libcamera release's
    per-suite tarball from github.com/Kurokesu/libcamera, verifies
    `SHA256SUMS.asc` against `.github/keys/kurokesu-archive-keyring.gpg`
    and extracts the `.debs`.
  * The build step feeds those `.debs` to `sbuild --extra-package`, which
    copies them into the chroot's temporary apt archive, and writes an
    apt preferences file (via `--chroot-setup-commands`) pinning
    `*libcamera*` to the pinned version at `Pin-Priority: 1001` so apt
    prefers it over any newer libcamera in archive.raspberrypi.com, even
    on a downgrade. libcamera carries an epoch, so the pin and the assert
    prepend `1:` to the epoch-free `libcamera.pin` value to match the
    installed .deb version.
  * After build-deps are resolved inside the chroot, an inline version
    assert (via sbuild's `--starting-build-commands`) fails the build if
    the installed `libcamera-dev` is not the pinned version. bookworm
    keeps the `~bpo12+1` suffix that dpkg records.
  * The resulting rpicam-apps debs runtime-depend (via `${shlibs}`) on
    the matching `libcamera0.7` package, so customers must install the
    corresponding Kurokesu libcamera release alongside.

  Retire when: never (the pin is the dependency mechanism). The file's
  value bumps with each libcamera resync.

Binary-set deviation (retirable)
--------------------------------

Trimmed to the stock Raspberry Pi OS install set
  Why:         ship exactly what `apt install rpicam-apps` pulls on a
               stock Pi OS install, for a strict drop-in upgrade.
  What:        dropped `rpicam-apps-hailo-postprocess` and
               `rpicam-apps-imx500-postprocess` plugins, `imx500-all`
               metapackage and transitional packages
               (`rpicam-apps-lite`, `libcamera-apps`,
               `libcamera-apps-lite`). `d/rules` sets
               `-Denable_imx500=false` and `-Denable_hailo=disabled`.
               `hailort` and `hailo-tappas-core` build-deps are dropped
               from `d/control`. RPi dropped tflite plugin and its
               build-dep themselves in 1.13.0-1, so that part of the
               trim is no longer a deviation.
  Retire when: Kurokesu decides to ship the Hailo AI HAT+ or Sony
               IMX500 AI Camera post-processing plugins as a
               version-locked bundle.

Per-suite build profile (retirable)
-----------------------------------

pkg.rpicam-apps.nolibav
  Why:         rpicam-apps 1.12.x's libav encoder requires the ffmpeg 7
               API (`LIBAVCODEC_VERSION_MAJOR >= 61`). bookworm ships
               ffmpeg 5.1, so the encoder cannot build there. Upstream
               keeps bookworm on the older rpicam-apps 1.9.x for the
               same reason. Kurokesu instead pairs bookworm with
               libcamera 0.7.x, so it builds the current rpicam-apps
               minus libav.
  What:        under the profile `d/rules` passes
               `-Denable_libav=disabled`, the `libav*` and
               `libswresample` build-deps are dropped
               (`<!pkg.rpicam-apps.nolibav>`), the `rpicam-apps-encoder`
               package (the libav plugin only, empty without it) is not
               built (`Build-Profiles: <!pkg.rpicam-apps.nolibav>`), and
               the `rpicam-apps` metapackage drops its
               `rpicam-apps-encoder` dependency. trixie builds without
               the profile and keeps full libav.
  Retire when: bookworm is dropped, or its ffmpeg is new enough for the
               libav encoder.

Patches (retirable)
-------------------

version-banner.patch
  Why:         the `rpicam-hello --version` banner is built from
               meson.project_version() (the bare upstream `1.12.0`).
               `utils/version.py` only appends the git short-sha and never
               reads the tag, so the banner cannot otherwise show the
               Kurokesu packaging version.
  What:        `meson.build` reads an optional `.tarball-version` file and
               uses it for the generated version string, so the banner
               reports the full version (e.g. `1.12.0+krks1`). The CI
               orig-tarball step writes that file (the upstream version
               from `d/changelog`) into the release tarball.
               meson.project_version() is left untouched, so the library
               soname (`librpicam_app.so.1.12.0`) is unaffected.
  Retire when: upstream reads `.tarball-version` (or equivalent) itself.

Seed
----

  * Source: github.com/raspberrypi/rpicam-apps, branch `pios/trixie`
    (rooted at upstream tag v1.12.0). Only the `debian/` subtree is
    vendored here.
  * Last seed: 6fa6207 (rpicam-apps 1.12.0-1).
  * Public branch stalled at that seed. Later RPi releases exist only
    as source packages on archive.raspberrypi.com, so cross-check
    `debian/` against `.debian.tar.xz` of the matching version.
    1.13.0-1 added Wayland build-deps and `-Denable_wayland=enabled`,
    carried here. It also dropped tflite plugin we had already trimmed.
  * Reseed procedure (in order):
      1. Replace `debian/` wholesale with the new seed's contents.
         Drop `debian/salsa-ci.yml`, which is Salsa-only.
      2. Reapply this file's deviations (identity, binary-set trim,
         libcamera pin file, and the quilt patches under
         `debian/patches/`). The wholesale `debian/` replacement drops
         `debian/patches/`, so restore it and re-verify each patch still
         applies zero-fuzz against the new upstream, refreshing it if not.
      3. Update the "Last seed" SHA above in the same commit.
      4. Bump `+krks<N>` and `-<M>` per the version scheme.
      5. Prepend a new `debian/changelog` entry summarizing the reseed.
      6. Build on every supported suite (see "Supported suites").

Releasing
---------

Releases are paired tags:

  * `kurokesu` is tagged with `v<upstream-version>`, revision-less,
    for example `v1.12.0+krks1`.
  * `debian/latest` is tagged with `debian/<full-version>`, carrying
    the Debian revision, for example `debian/1.12.0+krks1-1`.

Both tag names drop the version epoch (the `1:`): colons are illegal in
git refs, so `release.sh` strips it. The epoch stays in the changelog
and the built .deb only.

Packaging-only rebuilds (`-2`, `-3`) move only the packaging tag and
refresh the existing release page.

To release, run `./release.sh --execute` from this branch (no
arguments does a dry run). It derives both tag names from
`debian/changelog` on the remote packaging tip, reuses the source tag
on packaging-only rebuilds, refuses to tag unless the branch's latest
CI run is green and pushes both tags atomically. The packaging-tag
push triggers `.github/workflows/release.yml`.

Manual equivalent (what the script automates):

  1. Tag the source on `kurokesu` with `v<upstream-version>`.
  2. Tag this branch with `debian/<full-version>`.
  3. Push both tags.

New releases ship as pre-releases with skeleton notes. Fill the
sections by hand before promoting to Latest.

Release assets per build: a runtime tarball
`rpicam-apps_<full-version>_<suite>_<arch>.tar.gz` with the debs plus
the build's `.changes` and `.buildinfo`. Each release also carries
`SHA256SUMS` and a detached `SHA256SUMS.asc`. dbgsym debs and the
source package stay in workflow-run artifacts only.

Backport builds (bookworm) carry a `~bpo12+1` version suffix appended
at build time (Debian backports convention, matching RPi's own archive
practice). This keeps deb filenames unique across suites in a shared
APT pool and makes a bookworm-to-trixie OS upgrade prefer the trixie
build. The changelog, tags and orig tarball stay suite-free.

Editing rule
------------

Any Kurokesu-specific change to the recipe (`debian/control`,
`debian/rules`, `debian/libcamera.pin`, `debian/*.install` or other
files under `debian/`) must update the relevant section above in the
same commit.
